A1. The part of the world that the North Pole sticks in. ~ TOP
A4. When men have these they take a day off; when women have them they take a year off. ~ BIRTHDAYS
A11. It's not the overheads, but these kind of heads that make the Government so costly. ~ FAT
A14. Things that girls sometimes step into. (Slang.) ~ UNDIES
A16. The Russian national color. ~ RED
A17. How most of us would like to make money. ~ EASILY
A19. A sudden, uneasy scruple. ~ QUALM
A20. The universal feminine goal. ~ ALTAR
A21. Preposition denoting presence or position. ~ AT
A23. Most modern gold-diggers know very little about this. ~ ORE
A24. A period of time. ~ ERA
A26. When the light of a woman's life comes home this way the light is usually put out. ~ LIT
A27. The pronoun of one editor. ~ WE
A28. Preposition meaning from. ~ OF
A30. These are not so good. ~ EVILS
A31. The theoretical head of the family. ~ PA
A32. What most wives think that they have done for their husbands, all of their lives. ~ SLAVED
A34. A small insect, held forth as a moral example for grasshoppers, human beings, etc. ~ ANT
A35. Nickname for Estelle. ~ STELLA
A37. Vice-president Dawes was expected to make things do this in the Senate. ~ HUM
A38. One way to acquire a cellar. ~ DIG
A40. Hasten. ~ HIE
A41. Everything. ~ ALL
A42. Two-thirds of an ostrich egg. ~ EG
A44. This comes in handy when moving. ~ VAN
A46. It's a short road that hasn't one of these on it. ~ HOG
A47. Near. ~ BY
A48. Behold! ~ LO
A49. That which arises from roses, violets, cheese and fish. ~ AROMA
A50. Relatively speaking this is very close to your parents. ~ AUNTY
A51. The night owl's morning. ~ PM
A52. Like. ~ AS
A53. Any authentic vamp uses a tiger skin for this. ~ RUG
A54. One side of a question. ~ PRO
A55. Ease. (Super-simplified spelling.) ~ EE
A57. The kind of a maid that always missed. ~ OLD
A59. The world's a stage and a lot of people do this badly. ~ ACT
A61. Little things that stand in the way of great deeds. ~ IFS
A63. Woman was created after this; and has been after it ever since. ~ MAN
A65. A fat man's fox-trot. (We will now sing the duck song.) ~ WADDLE
A66. This is Greek "T." (Not the kind you get in restaurants.) ~ TAU
A68. This is something composed of a head and a lot of unruly members. ~ FAMILY
A69. A young author sends this on excursion trips (abbr.). ~ MS
A70. These are things people can pick up with their bare feet. ~ TACKS
A71. Preposition meaning toward. ~ TO
A72. Chief part of a question that perplexed Mr. Hamlet. ~ BE
A73. The President of these Benighted States. ~ CAL
A75. Something a young wife likes to buy for her husband. (Oh, well, it's just the first hundred years that are hard!) ~ TIE
A76. Unearned increment accrued by waiters, hat checkers, bellhops, barbers, cab drivers, etc. ~ TIP
A78. Second-hand cars are all right as far as they do this. ~ GO
A80. A street in Venice. ~ CANAL
A81. What dumb-bells are in London. ~ ASSES
A83. Glorified headpieces. ~ TIARAS
A85. Mr. Baba's first name. ~ ALI
A87. What husbands are when they fear to go home. ~ AFRAID
A89. A high explosive. ~ TNT
A90. A science included in the curriculum of all students of chemistry. ~ CHEMISTRY
A91. The translation of a woman's "may be." ~ YES

D1. A fish—this is very delicatessen. ~ TUNA
D2. Something you should keep if you want to succeed. ~ ON
D3. Abbreviation for very soon. ~ PDQ
D4. Something the lion seldom gets in his den (or elsewhere). ~ BEARDED
D5. A more or less poetic island. ~ ISLE
D6. An illustrious former President, soldier and hunter (init.). ~ TR
D7. A fish that gets pickled or kippered. ~ HERRING
D8. A degree. ~ DD
D9. College boys do this at a football game. ~ YELL
D10. To satisfy or indulge to excess. (Also an excellent Krazy Krack word.) ~ SATIATE
D11. A tree that sounds like a woman's coat. ~ FIR
D12. A famous governor. ~ AL
D13. Something typists never run to. ~ TYPE
D15. A variation of a common evidence of debt. ~ IUO
D18. What the ministrel men did, when the interlocutor said, "Gentlemen, be seated." ~ SAT
D22. A fragrant balsam obtained from a South American tree. ~ TOLU
D24. Topsy's girl friend. ~ EVA
D25. High in music (abbr.). ~ ALT
D27. A street full of money. ~ WALL
D29. This is about all that a soldier gets out of war. ~ FAME
D31. What the umpire means when he calls, "play ball!" ~ PLAY
D32. The more this way a bowl is, the fewer the soup. ~ SHALLOW
D33. The ebbing of the tied. ~ DIVORCE
D35. Most broadcasting stations should do this, permanently. (Two words.) ~ SIGNOFF
D36. Motto for men—"Marry in haste and repent with this." ~ ALIMONY
D39. A range (not cooking) or series (not world's). ~ GAMUT
D40. All good Mohammedans expect to see several of these when they die (sing.). ~ HOURI
D43. This should be stepped on lightly. ~ GAS
D45. When men have wives that do this they may not be bigamists but they do have one wife too many. ~ NAG
D46. Chance. ~ HAP
D47. Two of these make a farewell. ~ BYE
D52. Something a mathematician does when he wants to find out the total amount of his assets. ~ ADDS
D56. To discharge. (Not fire or can.) ~ EMIT
D58. The way the office boy's excuses sound on any day that there is a ball game. ~ LAME
D59. A book containing dates, formerly used instead of the weather man. ~ ALMANAC
D60. Little things that lurk in big kisses. ~ BACILLI
D62. What Chesterfields are supposed to do. ~ SATISFY
D64. The first thing that is made from a tree. (Two words.) ~ ALOG
D66. To work at tatting. ~ TAT
D67. An instrument of torture (abbr.). ~ UKE
D72. A target for jests; the offense of a goat. ~ BUTT
D73. Many a man gets a flivver instead of this. ~ CAR
D74. This also makes the mare go. ~ LASH
D76. The Czar of all the Russians. (Formerly.) ~ TSAR
D77. Prefix meaning through. ~ PER
D79. These are never even. ~ ODDS
D80. A sharp clawed or sharp tongued animal. ~ CAT
D82. To use words. ~ SAY
D84. Where politicians hope to be, after election. ~ IN
D85. In existence, or the jazz hour for starting home. ~ AM
D86. In existence. ~ IS
D88. Introduction for an explanation. ~ IE


